"""Multi-tenant integrity regressions.

Covers three audited findings plus their review follow-ups:
  - Agent control plane is tenant-scoped: the registry keys entries by
    (tenant_id, name) and every agents API operation (list/get/rotate/
    revoke/suspend/unsuspend/unregister/health) resolves only within the
    caller's tenant -- cross-tenant access reads as 404 (never 403), so
    tenant existence does not leak. Single-tenant behavior is unchanged.
  - Dispatch gates survive cross-tenant name collisions: identity
    verification, capability/scope filtering, and last-active tracking
    resolve the dispatched agent by OBJECT identity, so a second tenant
    registering the same name cannot re-open a revoked agent's gate,
    disable scope filtering, or misdirect activity tracking. A
    registered-but-unresolvable agent fails CLOSED at identity check.
  - Activity attribution: recorded activity events carry the caller's
    resolved tenant (request.state.auth_context), with a one-time inert
    warning when MULTI_TENANT_AUTH_ENABLED=true yet an AUTHENTICATED
    request still lands in the "default" tenant (unauthenticated
    fallback traffic must not burn the warning).
  - Four-eyes enforceability is explicit: CORRECTIONS_FOUR_EYES=strict
    rejects self-approval; warn (the default) allows it but logs loudly
    and records a "four_eyes_unenforceable" integrity flag once.
"""

from dataclasses import dataclass, field
from typing import Any

import pytest

from {{project_slug}}.agents.registry import AgentRegistry
from {{project_slug}}.learning.corrections import (
    STATUS_APPROVED,
    STATUS_PROPOSED,
    CorrectionsManager,
)
from {{project_slug}}.learning.four_eyes import FLAG_TYPE_FOUR_EYES
from {{project_slug}}.learning.store import SqliteLearningStore
